  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRising heights promote largely dry weather with slowly warming \ntemperatures through Wednesday. \n\nEnsembles continue to point at our next upper trough crossing \nthe area Thursday into Friday morning. This brings the next \nround of active weather across the forecast area, with POPs \nrising by Thursday afternoon. Differences in timing of the \ntrough will determine our convective environment and thus storm\nthreat on Thursday. At this time, machine learning models \nfavors a steeper severe threat east of the mountains closer to \nthe core of 30+ kts of shear. Despite this, CAPE values near \n1000 J/kg in the 50th percentile and 0-6km shear values near \n20-30 kts still point to a locally low but non-zero severe \nthreat, favoring damaging winds. \n\nEnsembles prog a decently quick trough passage, with rising \nheights by midday Friday. This likely kicks off another few dry\ndays with high pressure settling in. \n\nIn the long term, ensembles favor a flatter more progressive \npattern by late next weekend which could favor repeated rounds \nof storms. Cluster analysis of this pattern points to some \nsubtle timing and depth issues in the late week period that \naffect the weekend but still trends towards a more zonal (and \npossibly stormy) pattern by late next weekend.",
